35,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The usage patterns are uneven, and the history of the old fridge suggests a lack of shared responsibility for maintenance.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,It seems we have lost the spaces where we can truly connect or handle difficulties privately; our interactions have become quite superficial.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,It's interesting that we're spending so much time and money on outside rentals when the walk for a hot lunch is taking up a huge chunk of our day.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"The cost of outside rentals is a recurring drain, and the fit-out quotes show a significant price difference between a kitchen and a meeting space.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The physical reality is quite telling; the walls offer little privacy, and the lack of a proper eating area is spilling over into our workspace.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Servas, if the mess is spilling into the office, it confirms that the current lack of a designated purpose is creating an unsustainable environment.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"Exactly. If we continue to have difficult conversations in the open, we only further the distance between us. We need a space that allows for genuine, private human interaction.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, I agree that we need a better way to interact, but we can't ignore the time we're wasting. If we're spending twenty-five minutes just to get lunch, that's a productivity killer.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"Productivity is one thing, but the financial leak is what concerns me. We are paying for outside rooms for small groups, which is an inefficient use of our budget.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It seems we are all describing different symptoms of the same ailment. Whether it is the noise, the crumbs, or the cost, we are lacking a dedicated sanctuary for our specific needs.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"If we choose a kitchen, we risk repeating the fridge disaster. We need a system that ensures maintenance isn't left to a single person.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"A kitchen might solve the lunch issue, but it doesn't solve the need for a place to speak honestly and privately. A meeting space would address both the financial leak and our social distance.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, a meeting space doesn't help with the lunch commute. If we have a kitchen, we save time and money on food trips, which is a daily win for everyone.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, a kitchen is a one-time cost, but the outside hire is a monthly bleed. A meeting space stops that drain immediately and is a more rational investment for the company.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"We must also consider that a meeting space, if not managed well, may simply become another source of noise for those of us sitting nearest the door.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I believe a meeting space is the most logical choice, provided we implement a strict booking system to avoid the chaos of the current ""first-come, first-served"" arrangement.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,I concur. A meeting space provides the necessary privacy for the difficult conversations we've been avoiding and eliminates the need for external rentals.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,"I still think we're ignoring the daily grind. A meeting space is great for the budget, but it doesn't stop the crumbs at our desks or the long walks for food.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, the crumbs are a behavioral issue, not a lack of a kitchen. A meeting space solves a financial leak and a functional void simultaneously. It is the only rational choice.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,"I see the logic in a meeting space, yet I worry for the peace of our immediate surroundings. However, if it stops the office from becoming a dining hall, it may be the wisest path.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
